# Browser cache headers for the assets layer. Parsed by Workers at deploy time
# and never served itself. These rules apply only to static assets — responses
# generated by the Worker (all HTML) are untouched, so the `cache.enabled: false`
# reasoning in wrangler.jsonc still holds.
#
# Cloudflare's assets layer defaults every response to
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`. That is the right default for the
# unhashed files in public/ (favicon.svg, opengraph.png, manifest.json), which
# keep their bytes behind a stable URL. It is the wrong one for everything the
# bundler emits under /assets/: those are content-hashed (`name-HASH.ext`), so
# the bytes behind a given URL can never change and revalidation buys nothing.
#
# It costs plenty, though. The Geist woff2 is preloaded in __root.tsx and sits in
# disk cache after the first visit, but `must-revalidate` means the browser may
# not use its own copy until a round trip says so — measured at 1112ms and 140ms
# on two production loads, both landing after first paint. Until it resolved, the
# hero headline rendered in the metric-matched Arial fallback and then swapped to
# Geist, which is the flicker. The same round trip was also being paid on the
# stylesheet and ~57 route chunks, which is what made the font's queue so long.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
